****Topic: Data-binding

1. What is data binding?
- Data binding is a mechanism in Angular that connects the component's data and logic 
  with the HTML template displayed in the browser.

 Think of these four types as four tools. You don't use a hammer to measure temperature, and you don't use event binding just to display a student's name.

Binding type	        Direction	            Main syntax       	Purpose

Interpolation     	Component → template	    {{ }}            	Display a value as text
Property binding	  Component → template	    [property]	      Set an element or component property
Event binding	      Template → component	    (event)	          Respond to user actions
Two-way binding	    Both directions	          [(ngModel)]	      Keep an input and component value synchronized

Memorize the direction and purpose, not just the symbols. That is what lets you choose the correct binding when building a real application.


Practical uses:

    1. Interpolation: for displaying the text in the ui
    2. Property binding: Suppose you want a button to be disabled when a student is not logged in.
    3. Event binding: Now let the user increase the counter.
    4. Two way binding: Suppose the user types their name into an input box, and you want the component property to reflect the new value while also displaying it on the page.

  we must import the FormsModule in the component we are using to enable the [(ngModel)]


****Topic: Directives and Control flow

 What is directive?
 -A directive is an Angular class that adds behavior to an element or changes how an element is presented.

Imagine you're building a student dashboard. You need to:
- Display “Pass” when marks are at least 40.
- Display a list of students.
- Change a student's result color.
- Hide a login button after the user logs in.
Angular provides tools for these jobs so you don't have to manually manipulate the DOM for every change.

The DOM, or Document Object Model, is the browser's representation of the HTML page.


The three categories

1. Attribute directives

Change the appearance or behavior of an existing element.

Examples: NgClass, NgStyle.

Think: “Keep the button, but change its color.”




2. Structural directives

Traditionally, these create, repeat, or remove views.

Examples: *ngIf, *ngFor, and ngSwitch. (from the angular 17 it has modified as @if, @for, @switch)

Think: “Show the message only when the student passes.”




3. Components

A component is a special kind of directive with a template.

Examples: AppComponent, StudentCardComponent.

Think: “Create a reusable student card containing its own HTML and logic.”


Here are the common operators you'll use:

| Operator | Meaning | Example |
|---|---|---|
| > | Greater than | marks > 40 |
| < | Less than | marks < 40 |
| >= | Greater than or equal to | marks >= 40 |
| <= | Less than or equal to | marks <= 40 |
| === | Strict equality | grade === 'A' |
| !== | Strict inequality | grade !== 'F' |
| && | AND: both conditions true | marks >= 40 && isLoggedIn |
| || | OR: at least one condition true | marks >= 40 || hasGraceMarks |
| ! | NOT: reverses a Boolean | !isLoggedIn |

When should you use @if?
Use it when the UI depends on a condition:
- Login status
- Loading and error messages
- Pass/fail results
- Empty shopping carts
- Showing or hiding sections
- Permission-dependent UI

**Multiple cases with @switch

What problem does @switch solve?
Suppose you have a variable called userRole that can be "admin", "student", or "teacher".

You want to show a different dashboard message for each role.

You could write several @if conditions, but @switch is usually clearer when you are checking one expression against several specific values.

***Useful contextual variables in @for
Angular provides built-in variables that help you build richer lists.

Variable        Meaning
	          
$index	      Current item index, starting at 0
$first      	Whether this is the first item
$last        	Whether this is the last item
$even       	Whether the index is even
$odd	        Whether the index is odd
$count	      Total number of items


